<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Assignment 1: Hyperbolic Functions.

  Built from the problems marked for assignment work in the instructor's
  markup of the old Chapter 5.  The problems left unmarked went to
  review-problems-1.ptx.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 1</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems exercise the hyperbolic identities, the derivatives of the
hyperbolic functions and the inverse hyperbolic functions. If you would like
to review the material first, see
<xref ref="chapter-hyperbolic-functions" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-1">
<statement>
<p>
Show that <m>\tanh^2(x) + \operatorname{sech}^2(x) = 1</m>, for all
real numbers <m>x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We write both terms over the common denominator <m>\cosh^2(x)</m> and
use the identity <m>\cosh^2(x) - \sinh^2(x) = 1</m>, i.e.
<m>\sinh^2(x) + 1 = \cosh^2(x)</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\tanh^2(x) + \operatorname{sech}^2(x)
\amp = \frac{\sinh^2(x)}{\cosh^2(x)} + \frac{1}{\cosh^2(x)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\sinh^2(x) + 1}{\cosh^2(x)}
= \frac{\cosh^2(x)}{\cosh^2(x)}
= 1.</mrow>
</md>
Note that this is valid for every real <m>x</m>, since
<m>\cosh(x) \ge 1 \gt 0</m> and so the denominator is never zero.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-1">
<statement>
<p>
Compute <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\,3\sinh\!\left(\dfrac{3}{x}\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We use <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sinh(u) = \cosh(u)\,\dfrac{du}{dx}</m> with
<m>u = \dfrac{3}{x} = 3x^{-1}</m>, so
<m>\dfrac{du}{dx} = -3x^{-2} = -\dfrac{3}{x^2}</m>. Hence
<md>
\frac{d}{dx}\,3\sinh\!\left(\frac3x\right)
= 3\cosh\!\left(\frac3x\right)\left(-\frac{3}{x^2}\right)
= -\frac{9}{x^2}\cosh\!\left(\frac3x\right).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-2">
<statement>
<p>
Simplify <m>\cosh(\ln 2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using the definition <m>\cosh x = \dfrac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}</m> together
with <m>e^{\ln 2} = 2</m> and
<m>e^{-\ln 2} = \dfrac{1}{e^{\ln 2}} = \dfrac12</m>,
<md>
\cosh(\ln 2) = \frac{e^{\ln 2} + e^{-\ln 2}}{2}
= \frac{2 + \frac12}{2}
= \frac{\frac52}{2}
= \frac54.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-hyp-solve-equation">
<statement>
<p>
Solve the equation <m>5\sinh(x) - 3\cosh(x) = 3</m> for <m>x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We first replace the hyperbolic functions by their definitions:
<md>
<mrow>5\sinh(x) - 3\cosh(x) \amp = 3</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad
\frac{5\left(e^x - e^{-x}\right)}{2}
- \frac{3\left(e^x + e^{-x}\right)}{2} \amp = 3</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad e^x - 4e^{-x} \amp = 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Multiplying through by <m>e^x</m> and writing <m>u = e^x</m> turns this
into a quadratic equation:
<md>
<mrow>u^2 - 3u - 4 \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad (u-4)(u+1) \amp = 0.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Since <m>u = e^x</m> is positive, the root <m>u = -1</m> is impossible,
and only <m>u = 4</m> survives. Therefore
<md>
e^x = 4 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x = \ln 4 = 2\ln 2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-hyp-arctanh">
<statement>
<p>
Following the method of <xref ref="example-hyp-arccosh"/>, show that the
inverse hyperbolic tangent is given by
<md>
\tanh^{-1}(x) = \frac12\ln\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right),
\qquad -1 \lt x \lt 1.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Unlike <m>\cosh</m>, the function <m>\tanh</m> is increasing on all of
<m>\R</m>, so no restriction of its domain is needed. Set
<m>y = \tanh(x)</m> and solve for <m>x</m>. Multiplying the numerator and
the denominator by <m>e^{x}</m> gives
<md>
y = \frac{e^{x} - e^{-x}}{e^{x} + e^{-x}}\cdot\frac{e^{x}}{e^{x}}
= \frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Writing <m>u = e^{2x}</m> and clearing the denominator,
<md>
<mrow>y(u+1) \amp = u - 1</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad y + 1 \amp = u - yu = u(1-y)</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad u \amp = \frac{1+y}{1-y}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore <m>e^{2x} = \dfrac{1+y}{1-y}</m>, and taking the natural
logarithm gives <m>2x = \ln\left(\frac{1+y}{1-y}\right)</m>.
Interchanging the names of the two variables, so that
<m>y = \tanh^{-1}(x)</m> is the inverse function,
<md>
y = \tanh^{-1}(x) = \frac12\ln\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right).
</md>
The range of <m>\tanh</m> is the interval <m>(-1,1)</m>, which is exactly
the set of <m>x</m> for which <m>\frac{1+x}{1-x}</m> is positive, so this
is the domain of <m>\tanh^{-1}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-hyp-arctanh-integral">
<statement>
<p>
Use the substitution <m>x = \tanh(t)</m> to calculate
<md>
\int \frac{dx}{1-x^2}, \qquad -1 \lt x \lt 1,
</md>
and then use <xref ref="ex-hyp-arctanh"/> to write your answer with a
logarithm. <em>Hint:</em>
<m>\frac{d}{dt}\tanh(t) = \operatorname{sech}^2(t)</m>, and
<m>1 - \tanh^2(t) = \operatorname{sech}^2(t)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>x = \tanh(t)</m> we have
<m>dx = \operatorname{sech}^2(t)\,dt</m>, and the denominator collapses
by the identity in the hint:
<md>
<mrow>\int \frac{dx}{1-x^2}
\amp = \int \frac{\operatorname{sech}^2(t)}{1-\tanh^2(t)}\,dt</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \int \frac{\operatorname{sech}^2(t)}{\operatorname{sech}^2(t)}\,dt</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \int dt = t + C.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Since <m>x = \tanh(t)</m> means <m>t = \tanh^{-1}(x)</m>, and using
<xref ref="ex-hyp-arctanh"/> to rewrite the inverse function,
<md>
<mrow>\int \frac{dx}{1-x^2}
\amp = \tanh^{-1}(x) + C</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac12\ln\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right) + C.</mrow>
</md>
This agrees with the answer obtained by partial fractions, since
<m>\frac12\ln\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)
= \frac12\ln(1+x) - \frac12\ln(1-x)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
